Exercise 4.12 asks whether an approximable endomap \(f : D \to D\) must have a maximum fixed point, and asks for an example with many fixed points. On the two-token neighbourhood system of Example 1.2, `Basic` records that the singletons of the two tokens are distinct from each other and from the master neighbourhood. `idMap_fixed` says the identity approximable map fixes every element, because its elementwise action is the identity. `elemOne_not_le_elemZero` and `elemZero_not_le_elemOne` say the two total elements are incomparable: the singleton determining one lies in that element, so an inequality would force it to equal the master or the other singleton. `no_greatest_fixedPoint` assumes a greatest fixed point and places both total elements under it; the classification of elements gives bottom or one of the two totals, bottom does not lie above the total element determined by \(0\), and neither total element lies under the other. `example_412` is the conjunction of “every element is a fixed point of the identity” and “there is no greatest fixed point.” **Theorem:** yes — the identity on Example 1.2 is an approximable endomap with every element as a fixed point and with no greatest fixed point. **Proof:** yes — incomparability of the two total elements, together with the three-way classification, rules out a greatest fixed point.
